<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cesare Borgia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1475">1475</a>–<a href="/years/1507">1507</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Duke Valentino · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-borgia">House of Borgia</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cesare Borgia was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>. Made Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinois">Valentinois</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a> in 1498, he used papal money and French troops to conquer the lordships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a>, Forlì, Rimini, Pesaro, Faenza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a>, forming a Duchy of Romagna. His state collapsed after his father's death in 1503, and he was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia.jpg" alt="Cesare Borgia" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Bartolomeo Veneto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cesare_Borgia,_Duke_of_Valentinois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cesare Borgia was born at Subiaco in the Papal States, the illegitimate son of Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Rodrigo Borgia</a>, later Pope Alexander VI, and his Italian mistress Vannozza dei Cattanei. His family came from the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, and his great-uncle Alphonso Borgia became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-iii">Pope Callixtus III</a> in 1455. He studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was groomed for the Church, becoming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> and archbishop of Valencia while still a youth, and a cardinal when his father was elected pope. In 1497 his brother Giovanni, the captain-general of the papal forces, was murdered in unclear circumstances. A contemporary suggested that Cesare was the killer, but his part has never been established, and he had no certain motive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 17 August 1498 Cesare resigned as cardinal to follow a military career, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a> named him Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinois">Valentinois</a>. In 1499 he married Charlotte d'Albret, a sister of John III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, and accompanied Louis into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. With French troops and papal money, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a> and Forlì, which were ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caterina-sforza">Caterina Sforza</a>, and then drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-sforza">Giovanni Sforza</a> from Pesaro. Rimini and Faenza followed, whose young lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorre-iii-manfredi">Astorre III Manfredi</a> was later drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> on Cesare's order. In May 1501 he was created Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>. In 1502 he took Urbino and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a> by treason.</p><p>His condottieri, among them Vitellozzo Vitelli and the Orsini, feared his cruelty and plotted against him. On 31 December 1502 he called for reconciliation, then imprisoned them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sena-gallica">Senigallia</a> and had them strangled, a deed the historian Paolo Giovio called a wonderful deceiving. In 1503 he took the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-marino">San Marino</a>. Niccolò Machiavelli, who was at his court from October 1502 to January 1503, wrote dispatches on him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a> treats him as an example of the danger of gaining a principality through another's power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> served him as military engineer in 1502 and 1503.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a> named Cesare's dependence on the goodwill of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> as the chief weakness of his rule. His father died in 1503, while Cesare lay ill with malarial fever. Pius III, who supported him, reigned only twenty-six days, and Giuliano Della Rovere, his deadly enemy, won his support with promises he broke once elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>. Cesare was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, escaped from the Castle of La Mota, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> on 3 December 1506, where King John III welcomed him. During the siege of the castle of Viana he chased fleeing knights alone and was killed in an ambush on 12 March 1507. His remains lie outside the Church of Santa María at Viana.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cesare Borgia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1500 <b>Cesare Borgia</b>, son of Pope Alexander VI, subdued the lords of the Romagna with French help, and Pope Julius II personally led his troops, recovering Perugia and Bologna in 1506 and besieging Mirandola in 1511.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cesare Borgia</b>, son of Pope Alexander VI, resigned from the cardinalate to pursue a military career, the first person to do so.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-17">17 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XII later made it a duchy for <b>Cesare Borgia</b> in 1498, and Henry II gave it to Diane de Poitiers.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-valentinois">County of Valentinois</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1502 <b>Cesare Borgia</b> seized Camerino and had Giulio Cesare and several sons killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-camerino">Duchy of Camerino</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1499 and 1501 <b>Cesare Borgia</b>, son of Pope Alexander VI, conquered the small lordships of Romagna and the Marche, including Imola, Forlì, Pesaro, Rimini and Faenza, with French and papal troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-romagna">Duchy of Romagna</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1501 <b>Cesare Borgia</b> besieged Faenza, which surrendered; the young lord Astorre III Manfredi was taken to Rome and murdered in 1502.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-faenza">Lordship of Faenza</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Subiaco, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Viana, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.93,13.1&amp;year=1495" aria-label="Subiaco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4869 671 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cesare Borgia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5319 941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5319 941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.93,13.1&amp;year=1495">What was here in 1495? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
